  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Monday)...\n\nShort term forecast continues to be dominated by the broad upper\nlow centered over NC Montana this morning. Energy wrapping around\nthis system continues to produce widespread showers over a good\nportion of the Treasure state, while the dynamics associated with\nthe low pull plains moisture into the area from the southeast and\nprovide plenty of shear to support thunderstorm activity over far\neastern portions of the state. This system is forecast to pivot\nslowly northward over the next 24 hours and finally shifting \nnortheast of the forecast area by Wednesday night. Will continue \nto see unsettled conditions with mainly scattered \nafternoon/evening showers over the western half of the forecast \narea, with thunderstorms mainly confined to the eastern half today\nand tomorrow. There is a brief period of concern for strong to \nsevere storms over the far eastern counties (Fallon/Carter) from \naround 2pm through 6pm. Storms that do form could quickly become \nsevere in this area but will fairly quickly shift east into the \nDakotas. HREF is also showing a small cluster of storms developing\nearly evening over the Bighorn mountains, moving northeast across\nSE Montana through late tonight along an axis of favorable mid \nlevel instability. Models area hinting at the potential for a \nstrong storm or two with this cluster through the evening so \nsomething we will be keeping an eye on. \n\nArea gets on the downslope northwesterly wind side of the slowly\nexiting upper low tomorrow. This will bring a quieter day\nconvection wise, but still be looking at isolated to scattered \nshowers in the afternoon with residual surface moisture combining \nwith instability from the lower heights aloft. Can't rule out a \nweak thunderstorm mixed in but strong storms are not expected. \n\nUpper low moves well away from the area by Thursday, but its \nresidual impacts continue. Southeasterly low level flow increases \nWednesday night and continues gusty at times through Thursday. \nThis flow will advect in plenty of low level plains moisture. \nConvergence along this low level moisture advection axis along \nwith a weak disturbance crossing aloft will provide the forcing \nfor potentially strong afternoon and evening convection for areas \nfrom Billings east into the Dakotas. SPC currently has a slight\nrisk for SE portions of the forecast area for Thursday.  \n\nUpper low and its influences are finally out of our forecast by\nFriday with ridging building over the area. A strong Pacific storm\nsystem is taking shape for the PacNW heading into the weekend and\nour area will come under increasing southwest flow aloft with\nperiod of energy pushing over the area on this flow. This pattern\nis a favorable one for shower/thunderstorm development with the\npotential to move low level plains moisture deep into our area\nunder a strongly sheared atmosphere. A cold front is forecast\nSaturday night into Sunday which will provide additional focus \nfor convective development. Something to keep in mind if you have \noutdoor plans this weekend. \n\nTemperature forecast for the week trends from the 70s Wednesday\nand Thursday to potentially the mid 90s for Saturday ahead of the\nSaturday night cold front. Chambers",
